## Why two licences?

Software copyright law and hardware design protection are different.
The MIT License is written for copyrightable source code. The
CERN-OHL-P is written for hardware designs, which involve both the
copyright in the design files and the design of the physical artefact
they describe. Using each licence for the work it was designed for
gives contributors and users clear, enforceable terms across the whole
project — without imposing copyleft obligations on either side.

Both licences require only that you retain attribution and the licence
notices. Neither restricts commercial use. This matches the intent of
the project: the tools and the design are provided for others to apply,
extend, and build upon freely.

